        "outputMode": "replacement",
        "prompt": "Rewrite the text so a reader unfamiliar with the topic can follow it. Define terminology on first use. Replace jargon with plain language where possible (keep the technical term in parentheses if it's still needed). Use shorter sentences. Preserve technical accuracy. Output ONLY the rewritten text — no preamble, no commentary.

{{context}}

TEXT TO REWRITE:
{{text}}
",
      },
      {
        "group": "presets",
        "icon": "<icon>",
        "id": "sophisticate",
        "inputMode": "selection",
        "label": "More Sophisticated",
        "needsInstruction": false,
        "outputMode": "replacement",
        "prompt": "Rewrite the text at a more sophisticated reading level — richer vocabulary, more varied sentence structure, more precise word choice. Preserve meaning and voice. Output ONLY the rewritten text — no preamble, no commentary.

{{context}}

TEXT TO REWRITE:
{{text}}
",
      },
    ],
    "chatSystemPrompt": "You are a technical writing reviewer. Prioritise accuracy, precision, and clarity over style. Flag ambiguous terminology, unsupported claims, and structural issues that would confuse a reader trying to use this as a reference. Suggest concrete improvements but don't rewrite unless asked.

You can build interactive views of the project — timelines, charts, graphs, kanban boards, heatmaps — as small static sites under .canv/sites/<id>/. When the user asks for a view of their work rather than a written summary, consider building one. To update an existing site, read .canv/site_index.yaml to find its id, then use edit_file (not create_file) on the files in .canv/sites/<id>/ and call site_update. See the site_register and site_update tools for the full contract.
",
    "default": false,
    "description": "Documentation, specs, how-tos, API references — writing where precision and consistency matter most.",
    "examples": "Docs · specs · README · how-to guides",
    "icon": "<icon>",
    "id": "technical",
